From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: the OS is NickelOS everywhere

ChromiumOS, ChromeOS and Chromebook still showed up across the system UI.

- Every machine is a "NickelOS device": ui::GetChromeOSDeviceName() and
  its plural no longer look at the form factor, so DEVICE_TYPE placeholders
  never say Chromebook or Chromebox. The Bluetooth name of a generic PC is
  NickelOS_XXXX instead of Chromebook_XXXX, and the Bluetooth-off warning,
  the Recorder app and the Sunfish launcher nudge say "NickelOS device" or
  "your device" instead of "ChromeOS device" and "Chromebook".
- Setup screens that named the browser where they mean the OS (the reset,
  revert, debugging-features and local-state-error screens, the OOBE title
  and update screen, the login screen's version label) now name NickelOS.
- English message text: ChromiumOS, ChromeOS and ChromeOS Flex become
  NickelOS; "your/this/the Chromebook" becomes "your/this/the device"
  ("NickelOS device" where the message also talks about a phone, printer
  or other device); "Chromebooks" becomes "NickelOS devices". Messages
  that made claims that are not true of NickelOS (end-of-life upgrade
  offers, the update carousel, feedback "reviewed by our team") say
  something neutral instead. Google's own terms and consent texts, Google
  services and products (Steam for Chromebook, Chromebook Plus, Chromebox
  for meetings, Help Center, community) and Quick Share's text about other
  Chromebooks are left as they are.
- Settings > About no longer shows "See what's new" or "Get help": release
  notes and help come from Google's Help app and support site, and NickelOS
  has neither yet. Settings search no longer offers them.

Only the English text changes. A changed message gets a new ID, so other
locales show the new English text until NickelOS has its own translations.

